From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 12:00:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 09/11] fat: convert directory dates little-endian

The last field family in the FAT directory entry still exchanged in host
order -- and this is a restoration, not a new fix. The conversion was on main
on 2026-08-06 in 1772a01, in a patch that covered date.c, direntry.c and
ops.c together. Later that day c169cd9 cut 0008 back to its write half
because the read-side FIRST_FILE_CLUSTER substitution had measured 13 boot
failures out of 13, and date.c was deleted with it: same patch file, same
fate. That commit's own message says the date conversion "was measured too,
and is not what broke the boot". A patch file is the unit of revert, so
anything bundled into one inherits its verdict.

0008 stopping where it does is still right on its own terms. The date and
time words are *symmetrically* unconverted -- ConvertDOSDate composes a
native UWORD and ConvertFATDate is handed the raw field back -- so AROS
round-trips them among itself and the boot never noticed. Nothing else that
reads the card can, which is why it is still a defect: every timestamp we
write is byte-swapped on disk, and every timestamp written by anything else
reads back here as a nonsense date.

This is not a boot blocker and is not claimed to be one. The failure is
graceful by construction: ConvertFATDate range-checks the decoded fields and
substitutes 01-01-1978 when they are impossible, which a swapped date almost
always is. The visible symptom is uniformly wrong file dates, not a hang.

The conversion goes inside the two functions rather than at their call sites,
and that placement is what makes the change complete. Every producer and
consumer of these fields in rom/filesys/fat goes through ConvertFATDate or
ConvertDOSDate -- direntry.c:570,683, volume.c:506, ops.c:1179. The remaining
mentions of write_date, create_date and last_access_date are field-to-field
copies (Rename, ops.c:601-606) or local temporaries holding the on-disk
representation between a read and a write-back (direntry.c:568-578,
ops.c:1177-1183). None of them interprets the value, so none needs to know
the byte order changed, and the symmetry AROS relied on is preserved.

Also the last piece of the parked codex-2026-08-05 working state that was
neither a diagnostic nor superseded, which is how it was found again. See
AI_context/issues/ISSUE-0007.md.

Upstream defect on every big-endian AROS target; m68k-amiga, ppc-native and
ppc-morphos share this code.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 rom/filesys/fat/date.c | 15 +++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/rom/filesys/fat/date.c b/rom/filesys/fat/date.c
index 2cdd21c027..2880344bb6 100644
--- a/rom/filesys/fat/date.c
+++ b/rom/filesys/fat/date.c
@@ -24,6 +24,12 @@ void ConvertFATDate(UWORD date, UWORD time, struct DateStamp *ds,
     ULONG year, month, day, hours, mins, secs;
     struct ClockData clock_data;
 
+    /* The fields arrive raw from the directory entry, which is little-endian
+     * on disk whatever the host is. Decode before unpacking the bitfields,
+     * not after -- the masks below are defined on the FAT value. */
+    date = AROS_LE2WORD(date);
+    time = AROS_LE2WORD(time);
+
     /* Date bits: yyyy yyym mmmd dddd */
     year = (date & 0xfe00) >> 9;    /* Bits 15-9 */
     month = (date & 0x01e0) >> 5;   /* bits 8-5 */
@@ -93,10 +99,15 @@ void ConvertDOSDate(struct DateStamp *ds, UWORD * date, UWORD * time,
 
     /* All that remains is to bit-encode the whole lot */
 
+    /* Encoded last, so the caller may store the result straight into the
+     * directory entry. See ConvertFATDate for the other half. */
+
     /* Date bits: yyyy yyym mmmd dddd */
-    *date = (((ULONG) year) << 9) | (((ULONG) month) << 5) | day;
+    *date = AROS_WORD2LE((((ULONG) year) << 9) |
+        (((ULONG) month) << 5) | day);
 
     /* Time bits: hhhh hmmm mmms ssss */
-    *time = (((ULONG) hours) << 11) | (((ULONG) mins) << 5) | secs;
+    *time = AROS_WORD2LE((((ULONG) hours) << 11) |
+        (((ULONG) mins) << 5) | secs);
 }
 
